  "articlePanels": [
    {
      "kind": "lede",
      "html": "<p>People think ISO 27001 is a cyber standard. Mostly it is. But Annex A of ISO 27001:2022 also has physical and environmental controls. You cannot audit those from a desk. You walk the building and gather proof. This guide covers that walk-through half, and where WhatsApp fits.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "takeaways",
      "heading": "The short version",
      "html": "<ul><li>ISO 27001:2022 Annex A has physical controls under physical and environmental security.</li><li>They cover secure areas, entry, equipment siting and protection, and cabling.</li><li>You audit them by walking the building and gathering photos and documents.</li><li>Quickler suits the physical walk. It does not cover the IT or management clauses.</li><li>A qualified auditor and a UKAS-accredited body decide certification. Never a tool.</li><li>Proof tied to each control makes the gap clear.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Context",
      "heading": "Where physical controls sit",
      "html": "<p>The 2022 revision grouped Annex A into four themes: organisational, people, physical and technological. The physical theme holds the controls you can see and touch. They include physical security perimeters, physical entry, securing offices and rooms, protection from physical and environmental threats, equipment siting, supporting utilities, cabling security, equipment maintenance, and secure disposal or reuse.</p><p>These are not paperwork controls. An auditor confirms them by looking. Is the perimeter real? Does the door reader restrict entry? Is the server room safe from flood and fire? Is the cabling secure? So this half of the audit is a building walk.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Secure areas",
      "heading": "Perimeters, entry and secure rooms",
      "html": "<p>Start at the boundary. Annex A expects defined perimeters and controlled entry to secure areas. Check the barriers, the access control on sensitive doors, visitor sign-in and escorts, and whether the access list matches who can get in. A comms room door that does not lock is a Fault. So is an access list a year out of date.</p><p>Photograph the door and reader. Pull the access report. Record how visitors are logged. Each is proof against one control. Each beats a line in a spreadsheet.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Equipment and cabling",
      "heading": "Siting, utilities and cabling",
      "html": "<p>Equipment must be sited and protected from environmental threats and unauthorised access. Check the server and comms rooms. Is the kit away from windows and water? Is there backup power and cooling? Is fire detection and suppression in place and working? Power and cooling are a control in their own right.</p><p>Cabling must be protected from interception and damage. Look for cables that are contained, labelled and not open to casual access. For disposal, look for proof that drives are wiped or destroyed. You can see all of this.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Honest fit",
      "heading": "The walk-through half, not the whole standard",
      "html": "<p>Quickler helps with the physical walk. It records photos, access reports, readings and Faults against each physical control. It makes a tidy proof pack with a time on every item. That helps before an internal audit or certification visit.</p><p>It does not cover the IT and management clauses: ISMS scope, risk assessment, statement of applicability, access management, cryptography or management review. Those are the larger part and need a qualified assessor.</p><p><strong>Note:</strong> certification comes from a competent auditor and a UKAS-accredited certification body. Quickler does not certify anyone.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "faq",
      "heading": "Questions, answered",
      "html": "<h3>What physical controls does ISO 27001 Annex A cover?</h3><p>ISO 27001:2022 Annex A covers physical security perimeters, physical entry, securing offices and rooms, protection from physical and environmental threats, equipment siting and protection, supporting utilities, cabling security, equipment maintenance, and secure disposal or reuse of equipment. You audit them by walking the site and gathering proof.</p><h3>Can Quickler certify us to ISO 27001?</h3><p>No. No tool can. A qualified auditor at a UKAS-accredited certification body certifies you against the full standard. Quickler helps you gather the physical proof first.</p><h3>How do you audit ISO 27001 physical security?</h3><p>Walk the building with a competent assessor. Photograph secure areas and door controls. Pull access and visitor logs. Check where equipment sits, power, cooling, fire cover and cabling. Tie each piece of proof to its control.</p><h3>Does the 2022 version change the physical controls?</h3><p>The 2022 version groups Annex A into four themes, one of them physical, and merges some old controls. The substance is the same: secure areas, equipment protection, cabling security. The numbers and groupings changed. Check the current standard.</p><h3>Related guides</h3><ul><li><a href=\"/pages/data-centre-audit-software-uk.html\">Data centre audit software</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/comms-room-inspection-checklist-uk.html\">Comms room inspection checklist</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/dr-site-audit-checklist-uk.html\">DR site audit checklist</a></li></ul>"
